   167	
   168	## Relationship to the legacy design
   169	
   170	The legacy system's `git-clean-slate-plan.md` (read-only reference, `~/Projects/nedlern/docs/working/proposed/`) designed the many-writer version of this problem. This section is the one classification record for the rewrite policy (founding plan § Standing decisions), in its four-class vocabulary. This design keeps only two of its ideas, re-derived (**update-feature**): workflow rules expressed as CLAUDE.md documentation, and protection-as-lock reduced to one credential. Never imported (**remove-feature** — multi-writer machinery; this project has one writer): the three GitHub Apps, the credential helper, per-agent branches, the PR pipeline for ordinary work, the parking states. The repo's git config is minimal and stated here, not imported (**preserve-feature**, contract kept and values re-derived): `user.name`/`user.email` for the machine identity, `useConfigOnly` so no global identity leaks in. No consider-feature entries, so nothing goes to `legacy-feature-queue/`.
   171	
   172	## Acceptance tests
   173	
   174	Build sequencing is owned by `docs/issues/3-git-gatekeeper-build-slice-plan.md` (five slices, with the test↔slice map; this section's original one-task framing is superseded by it, ruled 2026-08-10). What remains here is the contract's acceptance-test index:
   175	
   176	T1 every form error refuses with its named error and no side effects · T2 happy path: the four success guarantees verified, trailer exact · T3 digest: identical resubmit answers `already-checked-in`; changed content digests fresh; metadata-only changes do not change the digest · T4 concurrent submissions (injected delay): winner clean, loser integrates over newer commits and succeeds · T5 conflict: same-content collision refuses with files + commits + next action · T6 retry cap: sustained head movement ends in `main-moving-too-fast`, never a spin · T7 crash recovery: worker killed mid-WORKING → `status` reports abandoned, resubmit sweeps and completes; killed after push → resubmit answers `already-checked-in` · T8 cancel: all three outcomes; cancel-after-push answers `too-late` · T9 advisory: undeclared worktree changes noted, never blocking · T10 retired (2026-08-10): its subject, the `imports` subcommand, was deleted — the trailer's exactness is covered by T2's trailer assertion and T11's screening · T11 each import error class refuses correctly · T12 retired (2026-08-10): its subject, the trailer-absence audit, was deleted from the design; slice 5's audit acceptance is B3c's three branch-protection-audit outcomes.
   177	
   178	## Open
   179	
   180	- **Cross-machine callers (C8):** the gatekeeper reads declared content from the caller's worktree, which requires a shared filesystem; Mac-side agents cannot be read from the Ubuntu box. Candidate shapes, none chosen: the caller pushes its branch and the request names a ref instead of relying on worktree bytes (a real contract change); Mac agents route work through the Ubuntu side; or a second credentialed host (weakens the single-place property of C2). Decide when a Mac-side agent first needs direct check-in.
   181	- The walked-approval evidence format (what walked approval looks like as a checkable artifact) — undesigned; slice 6 waits on it.
   182	
   183	## Resolved
   184	
   185	- Which artifact classes are gated on walked-approval evidence — the first class was designated (boss-ruled 2026-08-04): instruction-bearing text (CLAUDE.md files, skills with their prompt templates, injected system prompts, the wiki), whose check-ins require walked-approval evidence; class definition, procedure, and guards on [nedschorus#31](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/31). The check itself is slice 6, scheduled 2026-08-10 as a prerequisite of privileged-lane activation.
   186	- The fast-handoff S2 interaction — see § Cross-spec consequence (resolved).

