     1	# git-gatekeeper build — slice plan
     2	
     3	Issue: [nedschorus#3](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/3)
     4	
     5	Working material for the build of `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py`, the single
     6	program through which every change reaches `main` in nedschorus. This
     7	document decomposes that build into slices, fixes the boundary of the first
     8	one, and records the design points the specification deliberately leaves to
     9	the builder.
    10	
    11	**The specification is canonical, not this document.**
    12	[`docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md`](../cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md)
    13	(design-as-of 2026-07-24) states the contract; the build bindings in
    14	[`docs/issues/queue/3-gatekeeper-build-bindings.md`](queue/3-gatekeeper-build-bindings.md)
    15	(boss-walked 2026-07-30) supplement it with B1–B6. This plan only says what
    16	gets built in what order, and answers questions those two documents leave
    17	open. Where this plan and the specification appear to disagree, the
    18	specification wins and this plan is wrong.
    19	
    20	Author: choirmaster, session 1caf1c51 (first build task).
    21	
    22	## Why the order matters
    23	
    24	The gatekeeper is not an optimization; it is the project's only check-in
    25	lane. Until it exists, choirmaster is push-less by ruling
    26	([nedschorus#45](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/45),
    27	2026-08-07): it commits to its own branch and a Mac-side agent merges after
    28	review. The interim was ruled explicitly as "until the git-gatekeeper
    29	provides the check-in lane." So the first slice is chosen for one property
    30	above all others: **it is the shortest path to a change actually reaching
    31	main through the program.** Everything that does not serve that goes later.
    32	
    33	## The five slices
    34	
    35	| Slice | Delivers | Spec tests | Retires |
    36	|---|---|---|---|
    37	| 1 **BUILT 2026-08-08** | Synchronous check-in end to end: screening → candidate → commit → push | T1, T2, T3, T9 | the manual merge lane, on the happy path |
    38	| 2 **BUILT 2026-08-08** | The entry checkpoint: `--import` (the `imports` query was built here, then deleted by user ruling 2026-08-10 — `git log --grep` is the view) | T11 (T10 retired) | hand-recorded legacy imports |
    39	| 3 **BUILT 2026-08-09** | Concurrency: loser integrates over newer commits, real conflict refuses, retry cap | T4, T5, T6 | slice 1's `main-moved` refusal |
    40	| 4 | Worker lifecycle: `--no-wait`, detached worker, `status`, `cancel`, crash recovery | T7, T8 | slice 1's `unbuilt-option` refusal |
    41	| 5 | Enforcement surfaces: branch-protection audit, repo git config, CLAUDE.md workflow lines (trailer-absence audit deleted, user-ruled 2026-08-10) | B3c | the founding-window "boss watches every landing" guard |
    42	
    43	Not in any of the five, and deliberately so: the review-evidence check for
    44	the instruction-file class
    45	([nedschorus#31](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/31), guard
    46	1). It needs an approval-evidence format that does not exist yet, and the
    47	in-session tamper guard
    48	([`.claude/hooks/instruction-file-guard.py`](../../.claude/hooks/instruction-file-guard.py))
    49	covers the same surface today. It becomes slice 6 when that format is
    50	designed.
    51	
    52	User-ruled 2026-08-10: slice 6 is now a scheduled prerequisite of
    53	activating the privileged lane. The deployed, root-owned gatekeeper copy
    54	(C2 as amended) upgrades itself from main, which is safe only once the
    55	gatekeeper's own source is in the instruction-file class with the evidence
    56	check enforcing walked approval. Sequence: slices 4 and 5, then the
    57	approval-evidence format design, then slice 6, then the credential work.
    58	
    59	### Why the boundaries fall here
    60	
    61	- **Import is separable from check-in.** The import triple is three extra
    62	  fields, one extra copy step, and one extra trailer line — none of it on
    63	  the path a non-importing change takes. Splitting it keeps slice 1 from
    64	  carrying the legacy-repository fixture.
    65	- **Concurrency is the hardest logic in the design and is not needed to
    66	  land the first change.** Rebuilding a candidate over a moved main and
    67	  deciding clean-re-application versus real conflict is where the bugs
    68	  live. Slice 1 handles a moved main honestly — it refuses and teaches the
    69	  fix — and slice 3 upgrades that refusal into automatic integration. This
    70	  is the project's manual → script → automation ladder applied inside one
    71	  component.
    72	- **The worker lifecycle serves slow checks, and there are no slow checks
    73	  yet.** `--no-wait`, the detached worker, `status`, and `cancel` all exist
    74	  for a caller who does not want to block. In version 1 the pipeline is
    75	  screening plus construction plus a push; waiting is cheap.
    76	- **The audit is detection, not gating.** It catches the configuration
    77	  residual (protection drifting from the design; an owner credential
    78	  editing protection). Useful, but it protects a lane that does not exist
    79	  until slice 1 ships. (Its former sibling, the trailer-absence history
    80	  scan, was deleted by user ruling 2026-08-10 — see the spec's
    81	  enforcement section.)
    82	
    83	## Slice 1 — synchronous check-in, end to end
    84	
    85	*BUILT 2026-08-08. `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py` with
    86	`scripts/git-gatekeeper-test.py`; every item below is in place. The
    87	gate is working but dormant: this box authenticates as `ubuntu-claude`
    88	and branch protection admits only `NedLern`, so nothing checks in to
    89	the real repository until open item 1 is settled.*
    90	
    91	**Built:** `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py`, command `check-in`, `--wait` only,
    92	`--import none` only.
    93	
    94	In scope:
    95	
    96	1. **Instant screening, complete.** Every form refusal in the
    97	   specification's catalog that a non-importing request can reach:
    98	   `malformed-field`, `missing-message`, `unknown-path`, `unchanged-path`,
    99	   `empty-change`, `unknown-base`, `base-not-on-main`, plus B2's
   100	   `unsafe-path`. Each refusal names the error, the specific facts, and the
   101	   exact next action (B5's verb-first phrasing), and touches nothing.
   102	2. **The digest** (specification field 8): SHA-256 over base id, sorted
   103	   path list, each path's new bytes, and the import triple. Screened
   104	   against history at submit, so an identical resubmit answers
   105	   `already-checked-in <commit>` having done no work.
   106	3. **The resolve-once request record** (B4c): every environment-derived
   107	   field — origin above all — is resolved during screening and written into
   108	   the workspace as the request record. Nothing downstream re-derives it.
   109	4. **Candidate construction** in
   110	   `$XDG_STATE_HOME/nedschorus-gatekeeper/<digest>/` (B4a): a clone at the
   111	   declared base with exactly the declared changes applied. Unchanged files
   112	   come from main, never from the caller's worktree.
   113	5. **The commit and the full trailer block**, including B6's
   114	   `Gatekeeper-agent` line.
   115	6. **The push**, happy path.
   116	7. **The advisory** (specification § Constructive guarantees): undeclared
   117	   modifications in the caller's worktree are noted in the reply and never
   118	   block.
   119	8. **B1's reply contract**: one JSON object on stdout; exit 0 success and
   120	   informational, 1 catalog refusal, 2 program defect.
   121	9. **Workspace sweep** on both endings.
   122	
   123	Out of scope, each with the slice that takes it: `--import` (2), `imports`
   124	(2), automatic integration over a moved main (3), `conflict` and
   125	`main-moving-too-fast` (3), `--no-wait` and the detached worker (4),
   126	`status` (4), `cancel` (4), the refusal record B4d (4), the
   127	branch-protection audit (5),
   128	repo git config (5), CLAUDE.md workflow lines (5), review evidence (6).
   129	
   130	**Tests** — `scripts/git-gatekeeper-test.py`, matching the convention
   131	already in `scripts/`: standard library only, self-running, one PASS/FAIL
   132	line per case, non-zero exit on any failure. Cases: T1 every form refusal,
   133	each asserted to leave no side effect; T2 the happy path's four success
   134	guarantees with the trailer block asserted exactly; T3 the three digest
   135	properties (identical resubmit deduplicates, changed content digests fresh,
   136	metadata-only change does not); T9 the advisory; the `main-moved` refusal;
   137	and B3d's version-floor smoke assertion (Python ≥ 3.12, git ≥ the recorded
   138	floor — this box runs Python 3.14.4 and git 2.53.0).
   139	
   140	**The alternative boundary, considered and not chosen:** slice 1 could stop
   141	after screening and the digest — the whole pure core, no git writes, fully
   142	testable, roughly half the code. It was rejected because it delivers no
   143	check-in: the program would exist and the manual merge lane would still be
   144	the only way to main. The smaller slice is the fallback if slice 1 as
   145	scoped proves too large to land in one reviewable change.
   146	
   147	## Rewrite-policy classification for this slice
   148	
   149	Required by the rewrite policy (founding plan § Standing decisions): every
   150	piece of work that touches legacy material classifies the legacy features
   151	it touches. Two facts bound this slice's table.
   152	
   153	First, the classification's one home is the specification's § Relationship
   154	to the legacy design, which performed it at design time in the four-class
   155	vocabulary — this plan inherits those dispositions by pointer rather than
   156	restating them (deduplicated 2026-08-10; a table restating that section
   157	lived here until then, provenance in git history). Slice-landing notes
   158	unique to this plan: the CLAUDE.md workflow rules and the git-config pins
   159	land in slice 5. Second — verified 2026-08-07 — **the legacy checkout does
   160	not exist on this box.** `~/Projects/nedlern` is absent; only
   161	`~/Projects/nedschorus` is present. Slice 1 therefore reads no legacy
   162	material at all, and its classification is inherited, not fresh. No
   163	consider-feature entries, so nothing goes to `legacy-feature-queue/`.
   164	
   165	## Program follow-ups from the 2026-08-09 md-review
   166	
   167	Rulings from the user's review of the revised spec (records:
   168	`md-review-records/2026-08-09-git-gatekeeper-design/`); program work, not
   169	spec text.
   170	
   171	Applied 2026-08-11 (suite 140 cases green, was 146): the `imports`
   172	deletion, the base absorption, and the catalog collapse below — plus one
   173	stray the sweep exposed: the program's `unsafe-path` code, which the spec's
   174	catalog never listed, folded into `malformed-field` under the same
   175	collapse principle. The refusal-text quality pass ran the same day over
   176	the final catalog: every refusal site meets the user's bar (facts name
   177	the offending path/field and embed what the program holds — stderr,
   178	intervening commits, the current tip; next actions are verb-first and
   179	specific); no entry needed a user judgment call. Still pending, slice-4
   180	scope by design: the expiry sweep and the liveness check.
   181	
   182	- **Refusal-text quality pass** (user-ruled 2026-08-10). The bar, in the
   183	  user's words: a refused check-in gets "the best, most useful and
   184	  actionable refusal text that can be reliably generated." Sweep every
   185	  error-catalog entry against three questions: (1) does the text say what
   186	  happened, in the caller's terms; (2) does it name the exact next action,
   187	  not a category of action; (3) does it include every fact the program
   188	  already holds that the caller would otherwise have to dig up — the
   189	  offending path, the conflicting commit id, the expected form of a missing
   190	  argument. "Already holds" is the limit: no speculative investigation
   191	  beyond what the check-in computed. Entries whose text needs a judgment
   192	  call go to the user rather than being decided silently. Runs alongside
   193	  slice 4.
   194	- **Refusal-record expiry** (user-ruled 2026-08-10). Every gatekeeper
   195	  invocation first sweeps retained `--no-wait` refusal records older than
   196	  30 days — opportunistic, no daemon. Slice 4 work: it lands with the
   197	  machinery that creates the records.
   198	- **Liveness check before the resubmit sweep** (md-review finding HG31,
   199	  noted 2026-08-11). Concurrent identical submissions share one digest and
   200	  therefore one workspace; before sweeping a leftover workspace, test
   201	  whether its recorded worker is alive — alive answers `in-progress`
   202	  instead of sweeping the ground from under a running twin. Slice 4 work:
   203	  lands with the worker lifecycle it protects.
   204	- **Collapse the refusal catalog in the built program** (user-ruled
   205	  2026-08-10): delete the unreachable `empty-change` branch (dead code —
   206	  the first unchanged path refuses first) and its catalog entry; fold
   207	  `missing-message` into `malformed-field`; merge the four import codes
   208	  into `import-invalid`. Teaching text and facts keep every distinction.
   209	  Matching test-assertion edits across T1/T11. Apply after the review
   210	  walk closes, together with the other program follow-ups.
   211	- **Absorb the base into the program** (user-ruled 2026-08-10): drop the
   212	  `--base` field from the contract; the program computes
   213	  `git merge-base HEAD origin/main`, after a fetch, in the caller's
   214	  checkout. Retire `unknown-base` / `base-not-on-main`. Rework against
   215	  built code: screening resolves the base after `resolve_repository`;
   216	  T1's base cases delete; T3/T4/T5 fixtures set the caller's HEAD instead
   217	  of passing `--base`. Apply after the review walk closes.
   218	- **Delete the `imports` subcommand from the built program** (user-ruled
   219	  2026-08-10): the parser entry, `imports_table`, `parse_import_trailers`,
   220	  the per-call scratch clone, and T10's test cases. The `--import` trailer
   221	  machinery and T11's screening stay. The view is
   222	  `git log origin/main --grep "Gatekeeper-import:"`, documented in the
   223	  spec. Apply after the review walk closes; suite must stay green minus
   224	  the retired cases.
   225	
   226	## Design points this plan settles
   227	
   228	The specification leaves these to the builder. Each is a real fork, and
   229	each is recorded here as the answer the build will use.
   230	
   231	### D1 — Where the program reads main from
   232	
   233	The program clones `origin` into the per-digest workspace and builds the
   234	candidate there, at the declared base. It never uses the caller's worktree
   235	as the source of unchanged content, and it reads the caller's worktree
   236	exactly once, for the declared paths (specification field 1).
   237	
   238	Tests need this to point somewhere other than GitHub. Per B3a, pushing
   239	tests target a throwaway local bare repository, fresh per test. The program
   240	therefore takes the remote from an explicit argument that defaults to the
   241	invoking repository's `origin`, so a test can hand it a fixture path
   242	without any special test-only code path in the program.
   243	
   244	### D2 — Options not yet built
   245	
   246	Slice 1 does not implement `--no-wait`, `--import` with a value, or the
   247	`status`, `cancel`, and `imports` commands. Reaching one of them must not
   248	be an unnamed ending, and it must not exit 2 (B1 reserves exit 2 for
   249	program defects, so an argument-parser rejection would make a loop counter
   250	read a scoping decision as a gatekeeper bug).
   251	
   252	New catalog entry, `unbuilt-option`: a normal three-part refusal, exit 1,
   253	naming the option, the slice that builds it, and the available alternative
   254	("resubmit with `--wait`"). It is removed entry by entry as each slice
   255	lands, and is gone after slice 5.
   256	
   257	### D3 — The value of the `Gatekeeper-agent` trailer
   258	
   259	B6 requires `Gatekeeper-agent: <runtime/model>` on every commit, never
   260	omitted. The environment does not carry it: this box exposes
   261	`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and `AI_AGENT=claude-code_2-1-220_agent`, which
   262	name the runtime and its version but not the model, and the model is the
   263	half the fix ladder needs.
   264	
   265	The program therefore takes it as a required declared field, resolved once
   266	at screening like every other environment-derived value. The caller is the
   267	only party that knows which model it is. This is the cooperative class — a
   268	caller can declare wrongly — consistent with the design's honest-singleton
   269	stance: the gatekeeper records what it is told and never guesses.
   270	
   271	### D4 — A moved main, before slice 3
   272	
   273	Slice 1 pushes once. If the push is rejected because main moved since the
   274	declared base, the reply is a refusal named `main-moved`, carrying the
   275	files, the intervening commits, and the next action: update from main,
   276	rebase the work, resubmit. Nothing is left behind; the workspace is swept.
   277	
   278	`main-moved` retires in slice 3, where the same condition splits into
   279	automatic integration (clean re-application, the usual case) and `conflict`
   280	(the new main touched the same content).
   281	
   282	## Open — awaiting the user
   283	
   284	1. **The push credential.** Slice 1 ships fully tested against local bare
   285	   repositories, but it cannot check anything into
   286	   `nedschorus/nedschorus` from this box: the box is authenticated as
   287	   `ubuntu-claude`, and branch protection restricts pushes to `NedLern`.
   288	   The design's whole premise is that the program holds the project's one
   289	   push-capable credential. So slice 1 lands as a working, dormant gate,
   290	   and a separate decision — install the `NedLern` credential for the
   291	   program's use on this box, or run the program on the Mac, or move
   292	   straight to the dedicated-identity rung the design names — turns it
   293	   live. Not choirmaster's decision to make.
   294	2. **The `write-test-plan` skill.** Founding plan open question 8 names
   295	   [nedschorus#18](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/18) as
   296	   the first expected candidate-skill pull, triggered by exactly this task.
   297	   Building it first would produce this slice's test plan; not building it
   298	   means the test plan above is hand-written, as it currently is.
   299	
   300	## How this plan was ruled
   301	
   302	Walked with the user 2026-08-08 and closed at the first item by his direction:
   303	he is not picky about the slice boundaries, he wants progress, and he wants
   304	more than the first slice delivered. So the decomposition, the slice-1
   305	boundary, the classification table, and the four design points D1-D4 stand as
   306	written above — accepted by not being contested — and the build proceeds
   307	through the slices in order without per-item approval.
   308	
   309	Two consequences recorded here because they change what gets built:
   310	
   311	- The `write-test-plan` skill (open item 2) is NOT built first. Founding plan
   312	  open question 8 anticipated pulling it at this task, but building a skill
   313	  before building the thing it plans is the opposite of progress. The test
   314	  plans in this document are hand-written. The pull stays available the moment
   315	  a slice's test design genuinely stalls.
   316	- The push credential (open item 1) does not block. Slices land tested against
   317	  local bare repositories and go live when the credential question is settled,
   318	  which is the user's to settle.
   319	
