   214	  checkout. Retire `unknown-base` / `base-not-on-main`. Rework against
   215	  built code: screening resolves the base after `resolve_repository`;
   216	  T1's base cases delete; T3/T4/T5 fixtures set the caller's HEAD instead
   217	  of passing `--base`. Apply after the review walk closes.
   218	- **Delete the `imports` subcommand from the built program** (user-ruled
   219	  2026-08-10): the parser entry, `imports_table`, `parse_import_trailers`,
   220	  the per-call scratch clone, and T10's test cases. The `--import` trailer
   221	  machinery and T11's screening stay. The view is
   222	  `git log origin/main --grep "Gatekeeper-import:"`, documented in the
   223	  spec. Apply after the review walk closes; suite must stay green minus
   224	  the retired cases.
   225	
   226	## Design points this plan settles
   227	
   228	The specification leaves these to the builder. Each is a real fork, and
   229	each is recorded here as the answer the build will use.
   230	
   231	### D1 — Where the program reads main from
   232	
   233	The program clones `origin` into the per-digest workspace and builds the
   234	candidate there, at the declared base. It never uses the caller's worktree
   235	as the source of unchanged content, and it reads the caller's worktree
   236	exactly once, for the declared paths (specification field 1).
   237	
   238	Tests need this to point somewhere other than GitHub. Per B3a, pushing
   239	tests target a throwaway local bare repository, fresh per test. The program
   240	therefore takes the remote from an explicit argument that defaults to the
   241	invoking repository's `origin`, so a test can hand it a fixture path
   242	without any special test-only code path in the program.
   243	
   244	### D2 — Options not yet built
   245	
   246	Slice 1 does not implement `--no-wait`, `--import` with a value, or the
   247	`status`, `cancel`, and `imports` commands. Reaching one of them must not
   248	be an unnamed ending, and it must not exit 2 (B1 reserves exit 2 for
   249	program defects, so an argument-parser rejection would make a loop counter
   250	read a scoping decision as a gatekeeper bug).
   251	
   252	New catalog entry, `unbuilt-option`: a normal three-part refusal, exit 1,
   253	naming the option, the slice that builds it, and the available alternative
   254	("resubmit with `--wait`"). It is removed entry by entry as each slice
   255	lands, and is gone after slice 5.
   256	
   257	### D3 — The value of the `Gatekeeper-agent` trailer
   258	
   259	B6 requires `Gatekeeper-agent: <runtime/model>` on every commit, never
   260	omitted. The environment does not carry it: this box exposes
   261	`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and `AI_AGENT=claude-code_2-1-220_agent`, which
   262	name the runtime and its version but not the model, and the model is the
   263	half the fix ladder needs.
   264	
   265	The program therefore takes it as a required declared field, resolved once
   266	at screening like every other environment-derived value. The caller is the
   267	only party that knows which model it is. This is the cooperative class — a
   268	caller can declare wrongly — consistent with the design's honest-singleton
   269	stance: the gatekeeper records what it is told and never guesses.
   270	
   271	### D4 — A moved main, before slice 3
   272	
   273	Slice 1 pushes once. If the push is rejected because main moved since the
   274	declared base, the reply is a refusal named `main-moved`, carrying the
   275	files, the intervening commits, and the next action: update from main,
   276	rebase the work, resubmit. Nothing is left behind; the workspace is swept.
   277	
   278	`main-moved` retires in slice 3, where the same condition splits into
   279	automatic integration (clean re-application, the usual case) and `conflict`
   280	(the new main touched the same content).
   281	
   282	## Open — awaiting the user
   283	
   284	1. **The push credential.** Slice 1 ships fully tested against local bare
   285	   repositories, but it cannot check anything into
   286	   `nedschorus/nedschorus` from this box: the box is authenticated as
   287	   `ubuntu-claude`, and branch protection restricts pushes to `NedLern`.
   288	   The design's whole premise is that the program holds the project's one
   289	   push-capable credential. So slice 1 lands as a working, dormant gate,
   290	   and a separate decision — install the `NedLern` credential for the
   291	   program's use on this box, or run the program on the Mac, or move
   292	   straight to the dedicated-identity rung the design names — turns it
   293	   live. Not choirmaster's decision to make.
   294	2. **The `write-test-plan` skill.** Founding plan open question 8 names
   295	   [nedschorus#18](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/18) as
   296	   the first expected candidate-skill pull, triggered by exactly this task.
   297	   Building it first would produce this slice's test plan; not building it
   298	   means the test plan above is hand-written, as it currently is.
   299	
   300	## How this plan was ruled
   301	
   302	Walked with the user 2026-08-08 and closed at the first item by his direction:
   303	he is not picky about the slice boundaries, he wants progress, and he wants
